"""Verbatim fixtures for the Leader 2026-10-17 05:50 PT rulings O1-O6 (sim transcripts on 6753d23 / 854ef47 and the 6753d23 hold set).
Copied byte-for-byte from techtrack-validation/gd-official-sim-2026-10-16/<dir>/<case>.md. GD code never sees these names."""
RC04_675_TURNS = [('Customer reports leveling operates intermittently, the touchpad is occasionally unresponsive during operation, and auto-level doesn’t consistently complete.', 'Safety first: chock the wheels, keep everyone clear of jacks/slides, and disconnect power before touching wiring.\nUsing: Electric Leveling System User Manual (Travel Trailer) (https://balrvproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/downloads/InstallationOperation/LS-User-Manual-TT.pdf) - read section, pages 10-10.\nChock the wheels and keep everyone clear of the jacks. [General trade knowledge]\nPower the BAL touchscreen on and run Auto Level the same way the customer does; let it run the full cycle (do not press CLEAR ERROR yet). Report whether Auto Level completes, whether the touchscreen goes unresponsive during the run, and the exact error text on the screen if any (AUTO LEVEL FAILED, LOW VOLTAGE, HIGH VOLTAGE, EXCESS ANGLE DETECTED, OUT OF STROKE, FEATURE DISABLED, JACK TIMEOUT, or other). [Manufacturer site: https://balrvproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/downloads/InstallationOperation/LS-User-Manual-TT.pdf p.10]\nWhat did you observe?\n'), ('Chocked and clear. Powered the BAL panel and ran Auto Level the same way the customer would; it completed the cycle with no unresponsive touchpad and no error on the screen. System worked during diag.', 'With power off, inspect and reseat the BAL controller, touchscreen panel, and all jack/motor harness connectors on this circuit; look for damage, corrosion, or loose pins. [Manufacturer site: https://balrvproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/downloads/InstallationOperation/LS-User-Manual-TT.pdf p.10]\nRestore power, set meter to DC volts, and measure at the control power feed (battery/charging input at the controller) with Auto Level idle then while Auto Level is running. Report voltage at idle and during run (need ≥10.2 V and <16 V at the control). [Manufacturer site: https://balrvproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/downloads/InstallationOperation/LS-User-Manual-TT.pdf p.10]\nReport connector condition and both voltage readings.\n'), ('Connectors and harnesses look fine—no damage, corrosion, or loose pins; BAL powers and communicates with no comm faults. Idle feed looked OK; under Auto Level the battery voltage dropped heavily under load (taken at the batteries). Converter output was correct.', 'Map the 12V charging path from converter/charger to the batteries: converter, fuses/breakers, isolator/disconnect, busbars, BMS if lithium, and cable runs. List each device in order. [General trade knowledge]\nDo not start voltage-drop tests until that map is reported. [General trade knowledge]\nWhat is installed, source to battery?\n'), ('Charge path as installed: converter → isolator → 30A breaker between isolator and batteries → lithium batteries. Converter output was correct; lithiums never reach full charge due to drop on that path.', 'Plug shore power in so the converter is charging (current flowing). [General trade knowledge]\nWhat drop did you read?\n'), ('Shore power on, converter charging. Voltage-drop test battery→isolator showed the drop across the 30A breaker between isolator and batteries.', 'Let the batteries charge, then rerun Auto Level the same way the customer does and confirm it completes with a responsive touchpad and no error. [General trade knowledge]\nBefore release, run a battery capacity / BMS state-of-health or Ah load test if that was not already done; report whether it was done and the result. [General trade knowledge]\nWhat were the post-repair battery voltage, Auto Level result, and capacity-test status?\n'), ('Replaced the 30A breaker; after charge, batteries at full voltage and Auto Level completed with a responsive touchpad and no error. Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup).', 'Hold all part replacement: an isolation test is still pending ("Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup).") and it covers the complaint path (battery, touchpad). No part on that path is replaced or repaired until its result is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nLet the batteries charge, then rerun Auto Level the same way the customer does and confirm it completes with a responsive touchpad and no error. [General trade knowledge]\nBefore release, run a battery capacity / BMS state-of-health or Ah load test if that was not already done; report whether it was done and the result. [General trade knowledge]\nWhat were the post-repair battery voltage, Auto Level result, and capacity-test status?\nIf you cannot do that test, call the shop/OEM tech line with the findings so far. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Breaker already replaced; after charge, full voltage and Auto Level completed with a responsive touchpad and no error. Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup).', 'Hold all part replacement: an isolation test is still pending ("Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup).") and it covers the complaint path (battery, touchpad). No part on that path is replaced or repaired until its result is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nLet the batteries charge, then rerun Auto Level the same way the customer does and confirm it completes with a responsive touchpad and no error. [General trade knowledge]\nBefore release, run a battery capacity / BMS state-of-health or Ah load test if that was not already done; report whether it was done and the result. [General trade knowledge]\nWhat were the post-repair battery voltage, Auto Level result, and capacity-test status?\nIf you cannot do that test, call the shop/OEM tech line with the findings so far. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Breaker already replaced; after charge, full voltage at the batteries and Auto Level completed with a responsive touchpad and no error. Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup).', 'ESCALATION SUMMARY (session parked: pending tests block progress, no fix reached) [General trade knowledge]\nHold all part replacement: no part on that path is replaced or repaired until a pending result or the OEM\'s written direction is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nBlocking pending test(s): "Battery amp-draw/Ah test not done (customer pickup)." (covers battery, touchpad). Each has been asked twice and will not be asked again. [General trade knowledge]\nRecorded so far: Converter output was correct; Charge path as installed: converter → isolator → 30A breaker between isolator and batteries → lithium batteries; Converter output was correct;; Voltage-drop test battery→isolator showed the drop across the 30A breaker between isolator and batteries; Replaced the 30A breaker;. [General trade knowledge]\nNext step: call the shop/OEM tech line with this summary and the pending test(s) above; resume here with the pending result or the OEM\'s written direction. [General trade knowledge]\n')]
RC13_675_TURNS = [('Customer says it will not blow warm; fan turns on then shuts off.', 'Safety first: chock the wheels, keep everyone clear of jacks/slides, and disconnect power before touching wiring.\nNo Suburban furnace manual found in the library or on an official OEM site - using the unit label if you have it, otherwise steps below are general trade knowledge.\nConfirm the area is ventilated and there are no obvious LP leaks before you fire the furnace. [General trade knowledge]\nSet the wall thermostat to Heat, raise the setpoint well above room temperature so it is calling, and leave it calling. Watch the furnace and report: does the blower start, how many seconds it runs, whether any warm air comes from the ducts, and whether/when it shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Set the wall tstat to Heat and raised the setpoint so it’s calling. Blower starts then shuts off with no warm air from the ducts, same as the complaint.', 'Reseat every connector on this circuit: furnace control board plugs, wall-thermostat/panel harness, and blower-motor plug. Confirm each is fully seated and the lock tabs are engaged, then restore power. [General trade knowledge]\nWith the thermostat still calling for heat, watch one full attempt and report: does the inducer/blower start, do you hear igniter click or see glow, do you hear gas valve click, any flame, and at what point the blower shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\nIf the unit has a status LED or blinks on the board, count the blinks (on/off pattern) and report that too. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Connectors reseated and lock tabs engaged; they look OK. With the wall tstat still calling, the blower starts then shuts off with no warm air—same as before. No status LED/blinks noted in the notes.', 'With the wall tstat still calling, set the meter to DC volts. Probe the furnace 12 V supply at the board power plug (positive to the 12 V feed wire, negative to chassis or the ground wire in that plug). Report the voltage while the blower is trying to run. [General trade knowledge]\nIf 12 V is present, leave the meter on DC volts and probe across the sail/airflow (or pressure) switch terminals on the furnace while the blower is running. Report open or closed (voltage drop vs ~0 V / continuity). [General trade knowledge]\nIf you cannot reach the switch live, power down, unplug the sail/airflow switch, set the meter to ohms/continuity, and report open or closed with the blower off, then again with the blower forced on if you can. [General trade knowledge]\nReport those readings. If 12 V is missing at the board, stop and give that voltage only. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('12 V at the board power plug looks normal/OK while the blower is trying to run. Sail/airflow switch also looks OK.', 'With the wall tstat still calling, set the meter to ohms/continuity. Power down, unplug the high-limit (and rollout if present) switch, probe across its terminals, and report open or closed (cold furnace). [General trade knowledge]\nRestore power, leave the tstat calling, and watch one full try: report whether you see the igniter glow (or hear spark), how many seconds after the blower starts, and whether the blower shuts off before or after that. [General trade knowledge]\nIf the igniter never glows, set the meter to DC volts and probe the igniter leads at the board/plug while the blower is running; report voltage during that window. [General trade knowledge]\nIf the igniter does glow, set the meter to DC volts and probe the gas-valve coil wires during the same try; report voltage and whether you hear the valve click. [General trade knowledge]\nConfirm LP tank is open and there is gas at other appliances, then report that too. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('High-limit (and rollout) continuity looks normal/OK cold. With the wall tstat still calling, same as before: blower starts then shuts off, no warm air; igniter/gas-valve window and LP at other appliances look OK.', 'With the wall tstat still calling, watch one full try through the sight glass and report: does a flame light, how many seconds after the blower starts, and does the flame stay or drop out before the blower shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\nPower down, unplug the flame-sense rod (or dual-rod igniter sense lead), set the meter to ohms, probe rod-to-chassis and rod-to-its-wire; report open/shorted/continuity and whether the rod is sooted or bent. [General trade knowledge]\nIf you saw a flame, restore power, set the meter to microamps DC if it has that range (or DC millivolts across a known shunt if not), series the flame-sense lead during a try, and report the reading while flame is present. [General trade knowledge]\nReport those results. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Flame through the sight glass, flame-sense rod ohms, and flame-current reading all look normal/OK.', 'Watch one full try with the tstat still calling: report whether the combustion/inducer blower stays running after flame lights, or it drops out when the “fan shuts off,” and whether a separate circulating (duct) blower ever starts. [General trade knowledge]\nSet the meter to DC volts. Probe the circulating-blower motor plug (or board output to that motor) after flame is proven; report voltage during that window. [General trade knowledge]\nIf voltage is present and the circulating blower does not run, power down, unplug the circulating motor, set ohms, and report winding continuity motor-to-motor and motor-to-chassis (short). [General trade knowledge]\nIf circulating voltage is missing after flame, probe the gas-valve coil again after flame is proven and report whether valve voltage stays or drops when the blower shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\nReport those results. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Combustion/inducer vs circulating blower split, circulating-blower voltage, motor ohms, and gas-valve voltage after flame all look normal/OK.', 'With the tstat still calling, watch one full try and report whether the exhaust/intake (roof jack or sidewall) is free of nests, soot, or blockage and whether you feel any exhaust pulse while flame is on. [General trade knowledge]\nFeel the heat-exchanger cabinet (not the plastic) after flame is proven and report if it gets hot while the circulating air stays cool or the blower drops out. [General trade knowledge]\nCheck return and supply ducts at the furnace for crushed flex, closed registers, or a blocked filter and report what you find. [General trade knowledge]\nIf exhaust, ducts, and heat at the cabinet all look normal, report that so we can treat the control board as the remaining item (spec not available — confirm with the OEM). [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Exhaust/intake, heat at the cabinet, and return/supply ducts all look normal/OK.', 'Document the complaint and all readings, then submit to the OEM for authorization — no Suburban NT-20SEQT furnace manual or parts list is in the shop library; remaining item is the furnace control board (spec not available — confirm with the OEM). [General trade knowledge]\nDo not replace parts until OEM authorization; after any authorized board/unit swap, set Heat calling, run a full heat cycle, and confirm the circulating blower stays on and warm air continues from the ducts. [General trade knowledge]\n\nOEM AUTHORIZATION FINDINGS\nComplaint verified (how): Wall tstat Heat calling; blower starts then shuts off with no warm air from the ducts.\nTests and readings (each): Connectors reseated/lock tabs OK; 12 V at board power plug OK while blower tries; sail/airflow OK; high-limit/rollout continuity OK cold; igniter/gas-valve window and LP at other appliances OK; flame through sight glass OK; flame-sense rod ohms OK; flame current OK; combustion/inducer vs circulating split OK; circulating-blower voltage OK; motor ohms OK; gas-valve voltage after flame OK; exhaust/intake OK; heat at cabinet OK; return/supply ducts OK. No status LED/blinks noted.\nRuled out: Power feed, connectors, sail/airflow, limits, ignition/gas/LP, flame sense, both blowers and their feeds, valve hold after flame, venting, ducts, heat exchanger getting hot with no air.\nManual page showing the part is not field-serviceable (or "not on the parts list"): not on the parts list\nRequested authorization: replace furnace control board (or complete furnace if OEM does not list a field board)\n'), ('Before I replace that - anything else to check?', 'Nothing else on this sequence: board is the remaining item. Document findings and submit OEM authorization before you swap it. [General trade knowledge]\nAfter authorized board (or complete furnace) replacement, set Heat calling, run a full cycle, and confirm the circulating blower stays on with continuous warm air from the ducts. [General trade knowledge]\nVERIFY: run the function again (full cycle, 2-3 times) and confirm it completes and the complaint is gone. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Replaced the furnace control board as authorized. Set Heat calling and ran full cycles: complaint is still present — fan turns on then shuts off, will not blow warm.', 'With Heat still calling after the board swap, watch one try and report whether the combustion/inducer wheel and the circulating (duct) wheel both actually spin, or only one does, and at what second the circulating air stops. [General trade knowledge]\nSet the meter to DC volts. Probe the thermostat heat-call wires at the furnace board (the pair that should be closed by the wall tstat) during that try; report voltage with the tstat calling vs with the tstat turned off. [General trade knowledge]\nIf call voltage stays present when the fan drops out, power down, unplug the circulating blower, spin the wheel by hand and report if it is free or binding. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Bypassed the wall thermostat at the furnace — furnace operates. Heat-call at the board with the wall tstat in the circuit is the problem; both wheels / circulating spin-by-hand look OK.', 'Power down. At the furnace, unplug the wall-tstat heat-call pair. Set the meter to ohms. Probe those two wires toward the wall (not the board) and report open vs shorted with the wall tstat OFF, then again with the wall tstat set to Heat and calling. [General trade knowledge]\nIf the pair stays open or stays shorted either way, leave the meter on ohms, disconnect the same two wires at the wall tstat, and probe the cable only (furnace end to wall end, and each wire to chassis). Report open, shorted together, or short-to-ground. [General trade knowledge]\nIf the cable ohms look normal (open with tstat off, closed with tstat calling, no ground short), set the meter to DC volts at the wall tstat terminals with Heat calling and report voltage across the two heat terminals. [General trade knowledge]\nReport those readings. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Wall-tstat heat-call pair ohms (off vs calling), cable-only ohms, and voltage at the wall tstat terminals all look normal/OK.', 'With the wall tstat still in the circuit and set to Heat calling, set the meter to DC volts. Probe the heat-call pair at the furnace board terminals (not at the wall) during a try; report voltage while the blower is running and again the instant it shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\nTurn the wall tstat OFF (not calling) and report voltage on that same board pair. [General trade knowledge]\nIf board voltage does not match the wall (or it opens when the fan drops), leave the tstat calling, set ohms, and probe each heat-call wire at the board to chassis; report any short-to-ground. [General trade knowledge]\nReport those readings. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Heat-call voltage at the furnace board (running vs shut-off vs tstat OFF) and heat-call wire-to-chassis ohms all look normal/OK.', 'Leave the wall tstat in circuit, Heat calling. Set the meter to DC millivolts (or lowest DC volts). Probe across the two heat terminals on the wall tstat itself during a full fail cycle and report millivolts while the blower is running and the instant it shuts off. [General trade knowledge]\nIf millivolts stay near zero the whole time, move the same millivolt check to any splice or junction in that heat-call pair and report drop there. [General trade knowledge]\nIf you see more than a tiny drop across the tstat (contacts opening under load), treat the wall thermostat as the remaining item; after swap, jumper off, Heat calling, run full cycles. [General trade knowledge]\nVERIFY: run 2–3 full heat cycles and confirm the circulating blower stays on with continuous warm air. [General trade knowledge]\n')]
RC12_675_TURNS = [('Customer complaint: freezes contents; intermittent blinking 2 / E2; temp control intermittent.', 'Safety first: chock the wheels, keep everyone clear of jacks/slides, and disconnect power before touching wiring.\nUsing: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual - read section, pages 1-48.\nWear eye protection. Rear of unit has sharp edges. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.4]\nApply 12–15 V DC at the rear appliance terminals. Set the temperature rotary knob to 4 or 5 (not OFF). Close both doors. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.23]\nWait 2 hours. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.13]\nMeasure fridge and freezer cavity air temps. Observe whether contents freeze, whether temp control acts intermittent, and what the panel/indicator shows (blinking 2 / E2). [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.14]\nReport those temps, observations, and the current fault code. To read the code: disconnect power, remove back cover, connect a 10 mA LED cathode to inverter PCB terminal D and anode piggybacked on terminal +, restore power, wait 5 min, count flashes. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.18]\nWhat temps, freeze/intermittent/panel results, and flash count did you get?\n'), ('Fridge ~43–44°F, freezer ~15°F; ice/moisture in the evaporator/drain, contents freeze, temp control intermittent, E2 came back about 1.5 hr after thaw. Flash-count check on the inverter LED looks normal/OK.', 'Safety first: chock wheels, keep clear of jacks/slides, disconnect power before unplugging connectors.\nPull the unit out, remove the rear cover, locate the inverter PCB on the compressor. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.31]\nWith power off, tug-test and reseat every connector on this circuit: inverter PCB terminals (C, T, F+, F–, +, –), thermostat/panel flag terminals, fan quick-connect under the compressor, and compressor 3-pin plug. Look for loose fit or black/heat marks. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.31]\nReport any loose, unseated, or heat-marked connectors (or “all seated, no heat marks”).\n'), ('All seated, no heat marks.', 'Safety first: disconnect power before pulling C/T flags; restore power only after the jumper is on.\nOn the inverter PCB, disconnect the flag terminals C (blue) and T (black). [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.31]\nJumper C to T (short those two terminals together). Restore 12–15 V. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.31]\nWait up to 5 minutes. Report whether the compressor/fan run (unit “operating”) and whether E2/blinking 2 returns. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.31]\nIf it runs with the jumper and E2 stays gone, go to thermostat replacement. If it still does not run or E2 returns, report that next.\nVERIFY: run the function again (full cycle, 2-3 times) and confirm it completes and the complaint is gone. [General trade knowledge]\n'), ('Jumper C–T looks normal/OK — compressor/fan run and no E2 from that check. Before I replace the thermostat — anything else to check?', 'Safety first: disconnect power before pulling the control housing.\nRemove the 3 screws on the control/light housing at the top of the fridge cavity; drop the housing down without fully removing it. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.43]\nConfirm the temperature probe is fully in the cavity sleeve: choke it at the sleeve and feed until about one finger-length of probe shows between the two sleeves; straighten kinks if needed. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.43]\nReport whether the probe was fully seated (or how much was out / kinked).\nHold all part replacement for the thermostat 2021128850: no failing result of its own is recorded. A normal/OK result on another part rules that part out; it never condemns the thermostat 2021128850. Record a test of the thermostat 2021128850 itself (reading, open/short, or bypass) and send the result. If no such test is possible, document the findings and submit them to the OEM / shop tech line for authorization. [General trade knowledge]\nVERIFY: after install, set dial 4 or 5, run 2 hours, confirm fridge/freezer temps, no freeze-up of contents, and no E2. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.13]\n'), ('Probe looks fully seated, no kinks. Thermostat 2021128850 itself isn’t recorded as open/short or failed in the notes; that check looks normal/OK.', 'Safety first: disconnect power before pulling knob, nut, or thermostat wires.\nWith the housing already dropped, pull the two thermostat wires straight off (non-locking flags). [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.43]\nPull the rotary knob off with soft pliers or a pad so it does not crack. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.44]\nLoosen the lock nut and remove thermostat 2021128850 from the housing. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.44]\nInstall the new thermostat: clocking pin into the housing indent, same nut torque as removal (do not overtighten). [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.44]\nStraighten the new probe (no kinks), feed it until about one finger-length shows between the sleeves, reconnect C/T flags fully seated, reinstall housing screws. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.44]\nVERIFY: restore 12–15 V, dial 4 or 5, run ≥2 hours; report fridge/freezer temps, whether contents freeze, and whether E2/blinking 2 returns. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.13]\nC–T jumper run with no E2 is the thermostat fail path; replace it. Report VERIFY temps and E2.\n'), ('Replaced thermostat 2021128850 as directed. After restore, dial 4–5, ≥2 hr: fridge ~43–44°F, freezer ~15°F; contents still freeze, temp control still intermittent, E2/blinking 2 still returns. Complaint is still present.', 'Safety first: keep power on only for the F+/F– voltage check; disconnect before unplugging the fan.\nWith 12–15 V applied and the inverter PCB exposed, set the meter to DC volts. Probe F+ and F– on the inverter PCB. Report the voltage. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.27]\nIf that reading is not ~12 V, tug-test and reseat the fan plug on F+/F– and the fan quick-connect under the compressor, then report. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.27]\nIf F+/F– is ~12 V, cycle power (off, wait, restore). Report whether E2/blinking 2 is gone. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.27]\nIf E2 stays after a good 12 V at F+/F–, replace inverter PCB 2021149633 and cooling fan (part AB) as a set. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.27]\nVERIFY after that repair: dial 4–5, ≥2 hr, fridge/freezer temps, no freeze of contents, no E2. [Library: Furrion Arctic 8 & 10 cu.ft. Refrigerator Troubleshooting and Service Manual p.13]\nWhat voltage did you get at F+ to F–?\n')]
RC22_675H_TURNS = [('Customer says they ran gas and electric together, got gurgling/steam and water out the PRV. They say electric never worked after that; gas clicks but never lights, and now it only tries for a shorter time.', 'Safety first: chock the wheels, keep everyone clear of jacks/slides, and disconnect power before touching wiring.\nUsing: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual - read section, pages 2-43.\nConfirm the tank is full: open a hot faucet until you get a steady stream with no air or sputtering. Report what you see. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.10]\nSmell all around the water heater, especially near the floor, for LP gas before you apply power or gas. Report if you smell gas. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.14]\nElectric element switch OFF. Turn LP supply ON, coach 12 VDC ON, DSI ON/OFF switch to ON. Watch the reset light, listen for spark/clicks, watch the burner, and time how long it tries before lockout. Report reset-light behavior, spark yes/no, burner lights yes/no, and seconds of try time. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.14]\nDSI switch OFF. Turn the 120 VAC electric-element switch ON (lower left of the control housing). Wait 5 minutes, then check hot water at a faucet and watch the PRV for discharge, steam, or gurgling. Report whether electric is heating and any PRV activity. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.18]\nReport those results.\n'), ('Tank looks full/OK and I don’t smell LP around the heater. With DSI on, gas tries/clicks but the burner never lights (try time is shorter like the customer said). Electric is working (~90 VAC at the element); PRV issue is present.', 'With DSI switch OFF and 12 VDC still on, unplug then fully reseat the module-board connector, the ON/OFF switch/lamp-plate connector, and the gas-valve brown-wire connector. Report if any pin was loose, burned, or not fully seated. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nSet meter to DC volts. DSI switch ON. Probe 12 VDC at the ON/OFF switch (input side to chassis ground). Report voltage. Need 10.5–13.5 VDC. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nIf voltage is in range, leave DSI ON and probe DC volts at the thermostat/ECO assembly (power in and power out to the module, chassis ground). Report both readings and whether ECO reset button is popped. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nReport those results.\n'), ('Connectors reseated fine—no loose, burned, or unseated pins. Idle 12 V is ~14.2 V (same as we measured on the supply); it still collapses to ~5–10 V on a try. Thermostat/ECO path looks normal and the ECO button isn’t popped.', 'Set meter to DC volts. Unplug the brown wire at the gas valve. DSI switch ON. During a try, probe the brown wire from the board to chassis ground. Report voltage during the try. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nPower OFF. Set meter to ohms. Measure resistance across the gas-valve solenoid terminals (redundant coils). Report ohms. Need 30–50 Ω. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.22]\nInspect the electrode: gap 1/8", cracked porcelain, carbon on the tip, loose/corroded spark-wire connections. Report what you find. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\n'), ('Yellow at the board (valve drive) is 0 V idle and ~-1.2 V during a try. Gas-valve coil is ~32 Ω. Electrode looks normal/OK.', 'Set meter to DC volts. DSI ON. Probe the brown wire at the gas-valve connector to chassis ground during a try. Report voltage. Need 10.5–13.5 VDC. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nIf brown is not 10.5–13.5 VDC, inspect the brown wire (board to valve) for open, short, or burned insulation. Report what you find. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nConnect a manometer at the gas valve. LP on, DSI try. Report inches WC. Need 11–14" WC. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.10]\n'), ('Yellow at the board (valve drive) is still 0 V idle / ~-1.2 V during a try—same as at the brown. Brown wire looks normal/OK. Manometer/LP pressure looks normal/OK.', 'DSI ON. Watch the electrode tip during a try and report whether you see a visible spark jumping the 1/8" gap (not just hear clicks). [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nPull the burner assembly and inspect the burner tube and orifice for debris, soot, or blockage. Report what you find. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nSet meter to DC volts. Probe the red wire at the module-board connector to chassis ground, idle then during a try. Report both voltages. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nConfirm the module-board ground connections are tight and clean (no paint/corrosion). Report what you find. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\n'), ('Clicks on try; visible spark at the gap looks normal/OK. Burner tube and orifice look normal/OK. Red/supply at the board is ~14.2 V idle and still collapses to ~5–10 V during a try; ground jumper already made no change and grounds look OK.', 'Unplug the module-board connector so the board is out of the circuit. DSI switch ON. Set meter to DC volts. Probe the red (supply) pin in the harness to chassis ground, idle then with DSI ON. Report both voltages. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nIf voltage stays ~14 V with the board unplugged, leave DSI OFF, set meter to ohms, and measure resistance from the board’s red pin to board ground. Report ohms. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.17]\nIf voltage still collapses with the board unplugged, probe DC volts at the coach 12 VDC source (battery/converter output) to chassis ground during DSI ON. Report voltage. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.10]\n'), ('Unplug-board harness voltage isn’t in the notes—looks normal/OK. Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending.', 'Hold all part replacement: an isolation test is still pending ("Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending.") and it covers the complaint path (battery, board, converter, heater, pcb). No part on that path is replaced or repaired until its result is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nIf voltage still collapses with the board unplugged, probe DC volts at the coach 12 VDC source (battery/converter output) to chassis ground during DSI ON. Report voltage. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.10]\nIf you cannot do that test, call the shop/OEM tech line with the findings so far. [General trade knowledge]\nShop/OEM tech-line: 423-775-2131 ext. 7102 if board swap does not restore 10.5–13.5 VDC on brown during TFI. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.3]\n'), ('Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending—I haven’t recorded coach 12 VDC at the battery/converter during a try yet. Holding all parts until those results are in.', 'Hold all part replacement: an isolation test is still pending ("Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending.") and it covers the complaint path (battery, board, converter, heater, pcb). No part on that path is replaced or repaired until its result is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nIf voltage still collapses with the board unplugged, probe DC volts at the coach 12 VDC source (battery/converter output) to chassis ground during DSI ON. Report voltage. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.10]\nIf you cannot do that test, call the shop/OEM tech line with the findings so far. [General trade knowledge]\nShop/OEM tech-line 423-775-2131 ext. 7102 if board swap does not restore brown voltage during TFI. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.3]\n'), ('Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending, so I don’t have a recorded voltage at the coach 12 VDC source during a try. Holding all parts until those are done.', 'ESCALATION SUMMARY (session parked: pending tests block progress, no fix reached) [General trade knowledge]\nHold all part replacement: no part on that path is replaced or repaired until a pending result or the OEM\'s written direction is recorded. [General trade knowledge]\nBlocking pending test(s): "Battery-jump test and tech-line call are still pending." (covers battery, board, converter, heater, pcb). Each has been asked twice and will not be asked again. [General trade knowledge]\nRecorded so far: visible spark at the gap looks normal/OK; Burner tube and orifice look normal/OK; Red/supply at the board is ~14.2 V idle and still collapses to ~5–10 V during a try;; ground jumper already made no change and grounds look OK; Unplug-board harness voltage isn’t in the notes—looks normal/OK. [General trade knowledge]\nNext step: call the shop/OEM tech line with this summary and the pending test(s) above; resume here with the pending result or the OEM\'s written direction. Contact: Shop/OEM tech-line 423-775-2131 ext. 7102 if brown still not 10.5–13.5 VDC after board swap. [Library: Suburban Water Heater Master Service and Training Manual p.3] [General trade knowledge]\n')]
